<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Argentine National Anthem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words adopted 11 May <a href="/years/1813">1813</a>; music composed May <a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Himno Nacional Argentino</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">After the May Revolution of 1810, poets and musicians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> wrote a number of patriotic songs. The earliest, a &quot;Patriotic March&quot; published anonymously on 15 November 1810 in the Gazeta de Buenos Ayres, is generally believed to have words by Esteban de Luca and music by Blas Parera; it did not mention <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> or any independent state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/argentine-national-anthem.jpg" alt="Argentine National Anthem" width="330" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (Illustration) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Versión_francesa_del_Himno_argentino.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In mid-1812 the ruling triumvirate ordered the Buenos Aires Cabildo to commission a national anthem. A Franciscan friar, Cayetano Rodríguez, wrote a text approved on 4 August, and the Catalan musician Blas Parera set it to music and conducted the premiere at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> on 1 November. Less than a year later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assembly-of-the-year-xiii">Assembly of Year XIII</a> judged the song not effective enough, since recent triumphs of the Buenos Aires army had made the text obsolete. On 6 March 1813 poets were invited to submit lyrics, and the poem of the lawyer Vicente López y Planes was unanimously judged the best.</p><p>The words were designated the sole national march on 11 May 1813. Parera finished a new setting in a few days and conducted the official premiere at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> on 28 May, receiving 200 pesos. He possibly expressed his dislike of the new nation by quoting a negative gesture, in minor mode with dissonant chords, from an anti-Napoleonic war song.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Juan Pedro Esnaola's arrangement of around 1848 first used the title Himno Nacional Argentino. The full text, which López christened the Anthem of May, is ardently pro-independence and anti-Spanish, and its vision is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> as well as Argentine; a lion lying defeated at the feet of a new nation is an allegory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> itself. One line is disputed: an early song-sheet says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> opone, opposes, the front of the peoples of the union, while a corrected sheet of the same date has pone, positions itself at the front. The first reading has been seen as centralist, and also as a misprint.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The song became popular at once. Within ten years it was performed throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, where it was used until those countries had anthems of their own. In 1860 Esnaola was commissioned to make an official revision, with a slower tempo and richer harmony. A committee's version of 1927 was rejected after a heated public debate, and Esnaola's edition was confirmed as the official anthem in 1944.</p><p>The text was sung complete through the 19th century. On 30 March 1900 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julio-argentino-roca">Julio Argentino Roca</a> abridged it by decree, so that only the first and last verses and the chorus are sung at official festivals and in public schools, since Spanish immigrants now shared national life. No anti-Spanish text is sung today. The anthem is performed at official events and has an anniversary on 11 May.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Argentine National Anthem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
